# Test-Driven Development (TDD)

Write tests BEFORE implementation. Follow red-green-refactor cycle.

## TDD Cycle

1. **Red** - Write a failing test for the desired behavior
2. **Green** - Write minimal code to make the test pass
3. **Refactor** - Clean up while keeping tests green

## Test File Organization

```
src/
  components/
    ChatBox.tsx
    ChatBox.test.tsx  # Co-located test
  utils/
    format.ts
    format.test.ts    # Co-located test
```

## Test Structure

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - tests after implementation, vague descriptions
test('works', () => { ... });

// ✅ GOOD - descriptive, behavior-focused
describe('ChatBox', function() {
  describe('when user submits a message', function() {
    it('should display the message in the chat history', function() {
      // Arrange
      var container = document.createElement('div');
      
      // Act
      ChatBox.render(container);
      submitMessage(container, 'Hello');
      
      // Assert
      expect(container.textContent).toContain('User: Hello');
    });
  });
});
```

## Test Coverage Requirements

- Test all user-facing behaviors
- Test edge cases (empty input, long messages, network failures)
- Test Kindle-specific interactions (keyboard, navigation)

## Mocking External Dependencies

```typescript
// Mock LLM API calls
var mockCallCount = 0;
global.XMLHttpRequest = function() {
  this.open = function() {};
  this.send = function() {};
  this.onreadystatechange = null;
  mockCallCount++;
};
```

## Assertions

Use explicit assertions that read like documentation:
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
expect(result.isVisible).toBe(true);
expect(messages.length).toBe(3);
expect(error.message).toContain('Invalid input');
```
